{
  "pid": "public-medium-04",
  "source": "public",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "title": "Temperature Logic Control",
  "requirements": "Write a function block FB to implement a temperature control logic. The program should include the following functions:\n1. Read the value of the temperature sensor through an analog input module.\n2. Compare the read temperature value TemperatureSensorInput with the set temperature value SetTemperature.\n3. If the actual temperature is lower than the set temperature, start the heater HeaterOutput.\n4. If the actual temperature is higher than the set temperature, turn off the heater HeaterOutput.\n5. One scan cycle represents 1 s (this platform has no TIME type, so every duration below is counted in scan cycles).\n5.1 A heating demand exists only when the reading is valid AND strictly lower than SetTemperature. A reading equal to SetTemperature, or a sensor fault, means no demand and also resets the continuous-heating counter.\n5.2 Overheating protection triggers on the 5th consecutive demand cycle (i.e. 5 s), and the heater is already OFF on that very cycle. \"Continuously for 5 cycles\" means the counter reaching 5, not 6.\n5.3 Cooling starts on the next cycle. The first two cycles stay OFF; on the third cooling cycle the protection is lifted and the decision is made again from that cycle's temperature.\n5.4 The cooling counter is independent of the temperature reading and of the fault flag: a fault during cooling does not reset or lengthen the cooling period.\n5.5 If heating restarts on the cycle where protection is lifted, that cycle counts as cycle 1 of the next round.\n5.6 Consequently, with a permanently low reading the heater is ON for cycles 1-4, OFF for cycles 5-7, and ON again from cycle 8.\n5.7 The cycle on which protection is lifted must NOT heat if the reading equals SetTemperature or the sensor is faulty — protection is lifted either way, the heater stays OFF.\n6. Provide status indication, displaying the current temperature, set temperature, heater status, and whether overheating protection is triggered.\n7. Provide temperature sensor fault judgment, if the read temperature value TemperatureSensorInput is less than 0 or greater than 100, it is considered a sensor fault, and the heater should be turned off.\nInitial state: heater output and status are FALSE, protection is FALSE, and both internal counters are zero.",
